  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts a messy, poorly resolved view of the soil microbiome with a more organized, mechanistic understanding that researchers want to achieve. At the top, soil beneath trees is linked to a CO₂ cycle symbol, emphasizing the microbiome’s role in carbon cycling and climate-relevant processes.\n\nOn the left, the “current state” is shown as a crowded box of many differently shaped and colored microbes embedded in soil, suggesting a highly diverse community whose members and functions are difficult to disentangle. The accompanying points indicate that sequencing can reveal which organisms are present and hint at their possible functions, but that this still makes it hard to connect community composition to bulk processes like soil respiration. It also notes that many genes, proteins, and metabolites involved in carbon and nutrient metabolism remain unknown.\n\nOn the right, the “desired outcome” is represented as a network diagram with interconnected boxes and arrows around a central metabolic hub. This conveys the goal of mapping the biochemical pathways and molecular interactions that drive soil carbon and nutrient cycling. The text emphasizes using this knowledge to predict climate change effects on metabolism and to define the soil microbiome’s “metaphenome” under different environmental conditions.",
